\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

When language models train iteratively on their own output, the training distribution progressively loses its tail modes, a phenomenon termed model collapse~\cite{shumailov2024curse,alemohammad2024mad}.
Lexical diversity narrows, calibration deteriorates, and each generation inherits the systematic biases of its predecessors.
As machine-generated text becomes prevalent on the web, such feedback loops pose a growing and often invisible risk to production training pipelines.
By the time standard quality metrics register degradation, the underlying distribution may have already shifted irreversibly.
This threat motivates the search for cheap-to-compute \emph{canary metrics}: leading indicators that signal distributional degradation before expensive downstream measures reveal the full extent of collapse.
The concept draws on the early-warning paradigm~\cite{scheffer2009early}, though our detection mechanism---Granger-causal temporal precedence---differs from critical-slowing-down indicators, as model collapse is a first-order transition~\cite{truong2026phase} (\S\ref{sec:related_work}).

Yet a fundamental question remains unanswered: \emph{at what contamination level does Granger-causal detection (temporal predictive precedence, not true causation) of collapse become possible?}
Whether a candidate canary genuinely \emph{precedes} downstream degradation or merely correlates with it under shared non-stationary trends depends on contamination regime and statistical method in ways that existing analyses leave unresolved.
Iterative training produces short, noisy time series (often fewer than 15 points per generation sequence), where detection power drops sharply and false positive rates can be high~\cite{boettiger2012limits}.

Prior work characterizes collapse mechanisms and scaling laws~\cite{shumailov2024curse,dohmatob2025strong}; we complement this by developing a statistical monitoring framework, making four design choices absent from existing monitoring analyses:
(1)~differencing to rule out spurious regression from shared non-stationary trends~\cite{granger1974spurious};
(2)~systematic comparison across multiple statistical methods rather than reliance on a single approach;
(3)~FDR correction across simultaneously tested metric pairs;
(4)~regime-dependent analysis across contamination levels.

Using Pythia-410M~\cite{biderman2023pythia} across thirteen contamination levels and up to four seeds, we find that PermG reveals a detection phase transition at $\alpha_c \approx 0.50$, with cross-architecture validation across five families (345M--6.9B).
Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality~\cite{shojaie2022granger}; our canary claim is scoped to diversity metrics (distinct-$n$~\cite{li2016diversity}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Method sensitivity as a primary finding} (\S\ref{sec:mmct}--\ref{sec:dose_response}).
    Five detection methods applied to identical data yield null-control FPR from 0\% to 92\%; the same Granger test shifts from 0/8 significant pairs to 6/8 across a 5-percentage-point contamination boundary.
    Method choice alone can shift conclusions from universal significance to complete null.

    \item \textbf{Cascade ordering with empirical validation} (\S\ref{sec:cascade}).
    Under mode amplification, entropy Granger-precedes diversity (Proposition~\ref{thm:entropy_leads}), with a ${\geq}1$-generation lag (Proposition~\ref{prop:div_lag}), confirmed in 15/15 experiments ($\alpha \geq 0.30$).

    \item \textbf{Detection boundary characterization and intervention} (\S\ref{sec:regime}--\ref{sec:intervention}).
    PermG undergoes a detection phase transition at $\alpha_c \approx 0.50$ ($T{=}10$); $T{=}20$ extends detection to $\alpha{=}0.45$.
    Canary-triggered cessation reduces peak perplexity by 58\% (proof-of-concept).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{PermG forward detection rate across 13 contamination levels (Pythia-410M, fp32, direction-checked). Four regimes emerge: blind ($\alpha \leq 0.45$, 0/8), onset ($\alpha{=}0.50$, 6/8), synchronous dip ($\alpha{=}0.60$, 0/8), and detection ($\alpha \geq 0.75$, 0--6/8). The onset at $\alpha_c{=}0.483$ is detailed in \S\ref{sec:dose_response}.}
\label{fig:regime_map}
\end{figure*}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and canary metrics.}
Iterative training collapse was formalized by \citet{shumailov2024curse} and \citet{alemohammad2024mad}, who showed that recursively replacing real data with model-generated text causes progressive tail erosion; \citet{schaeffer2025position} systematize eight distinct notions of ``collapse'' that this literature conflates.
Theoretical analyses have sharpened these results: \citet{dohmatob2024tale} recast collapse as a change in scaling-law exponents, \citet{dohmatob2025strong} proved that collapse can occur even at vanishingly small synthetic fractions, \citet{seddik2025statistical} provide a statistical analysis of iterative contamination dynamics, and \citet{marchi2025challenging} challenge the universality of collapse under diverse training conditions.
Several works have proposed monitoring metrics: \citet{du2026geometric} observe that calibration error rises before accuracy degrades, \citet{du2025scales} report ECE increases at 30\% contamination before accuracy drops, \citet{gambetta2024surplexity} introduces surplexity as a collapse detector, and \citet{drayson2025detection} show that machine-generated text detection can prevent collapse, and \citet{li2025verification} propose verification-based mitigation.
We differ from the closest predecessor, \citet{du2026geometric}, in four respects: (i)~we compare an entire family of canary metrics; (ii)~we cross-validate across five statistical methods; (iii)~we map detectability as a function of contamination regime; and (iv)~we treat method-selection sensitivity itself as a primary finding.

\paragraph{Data mixing phase transitions.}
\citet{kazdan2025collapse} map the conditions under which synthetic data triggers collapse versus improves performance, \citet{gu2024cmr} establish a scaling law for critical mixture ratios in continual pre-training, and \citet{gu2025datamixing} show that data mixing can induce phase transitions in knowledge acquisition---a direct theoretical analogue to our detection phase transition, where a critical mixing ratio separates qualitatively different detection regimes.

\paragraph{Critical transitions and lead-lag methodology.}
\citet{scheffer2009early} established that systems approaching tipping points exhibit generic early-warning signals (rising autocorrelation and variance); \citet{dakos2012methods} developed a practical toolkit for estimating such signals, while \citet{boettiger2012limits} quantified detection limits in short time series---directly relevant to iterative training where $T{=}5$--$20$.
Crucially, \citet{truong2026phase} prove that entropy collapse in iterative training is a first-order (discontinuous) phase transition, structurally ruling out critical-slowing-down (CSD) early-warning signals; \citet{buffa2026entropy} independently propose token entropy as a monitoring metric.
Our ``detection onset'' is a \emph{statistical} threshold (PermG power crossing a detection floor), not a thermodynamic phase transition, and our cascade ordering describes within-generation temporal ordering of metrics, not CSD-type precursors.
The standard framework for testing temporal precedence is Granger causality~\cite{granger1969investigating}, with neural extensions for nonlinear settings~\citep{marcinkevics2022neural}; however, non-stationary series inflate rejection rates~\cite{granger1974spurious}, and the Toda--Yamamoto procedure~\cite{toda1995statistical} addresses this at reduced degrees of freedom severe in short series.
These methods are largely absent from the model collapse literature.

\paragraph{Analytical robustness.}
\citet{bell2022modeling} introduced multiverse analysis to ML, \citet{coretta2023multidimensional} demonstrated that 46 teams reached different conclusions on identical data, and \citet{kapoor2024reforms} synthesized these concerns into a consensus checklist.
Our work applies this perspective to model collapse: five methods across multiple contamination regimes produce conclusions ranging from universal significance to complete null.

\section{Cascade Ordering}
\label{sec:cascade}

We formalize the empirical regularity that entropy is the earliest available canary metric for diversity collapse.
Under mode amplification---the assumption that MLE on peaked distributions concentrates probability toward high-frequency tokens~\cite{shumailov2024curse,dohmatob2024tale}---we derive a formal ordering of metric responses with theoretical grounding (proofs in Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).

\begin{proposition}[Entropy leads diversity]\label{thm:entropy_leads}
Under mode amplification: \textup{(a)} entropy can decrease while the $\epsilon$-effective support $S_\epsilon(p) {=} |\{i: p_i > \epsilon\}|$ (hence distinct-$n$) is unchanged; \textup{(b)} if distinct-$n$ decreases, entropy has already decreased.
\end{proposition}

The asymmetry arises because entropy responds to any mass redistribution (first-order), while diversity changes only when a token probability crosses the generation threshold~$\epsilon$; the margin $\gamma = \min_{i: p_i > \epsilon}(p_i - \epsilon)$ quantifies how much shift diversity absorbs silently.

\begin{proposition}[Diversity lag $\geq 1$ generation]\label{prop:div_lag}
If the per-generation perturbation satisfies $\alpha\|\mathcal{T}(p_t) - p_t\|_\infty < \gamma(p_t, \epsilon)$ through entropy onset $t_H$, then diversity onset $t_D \geq t_H + 1$.
\end{proposition}

ECE co-moves with entropy at zero lag: peakier conditionals simultaneously decrease per-token entropy and increase confidence ($\Delta\mathrm{ECE} \approx \Delta C > 0$ whenever $\Delta\bar{H} < 0$; qualitative argument, Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).
The predicted cascade $\{H, \mathrm{ECE}\} \to \mathrm{Distinct}$-$n$ is confirmed in 15/15 experiments at $\alpha \geq 0.30$ (within-domain WikiText, $2\sigma$ onset threshold): entropy and ECE onset at generation~1, distinct-$n$ at generation~2 ($t_H{=}t_{\mathrm{ECE}}{=}1$, $t_D{=}2$ uniformly; \S\ref{sec:regime}; Appendix~\ref{app:moment_cascade}).
Five low-$\alpha$ experiments show no detectable onset, leaving the ordering undefined below the detection floor.
The cascade establishes entropy as the \emph{optimal canary metric}---the earliest available signal---and explains PermG's directional selectivity: forward tests detect temporal precedence (6/8) while reverse tests find none (0/8) at $\alpha \geq 0.50$.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Granger precedence is not causation.}
Granger causality tests predictive improvement, not true causation~\cite{granger1969investigating,shojaie2022granger}; our claim is scoped accordingly---``token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse''---and bivariate tests cannot exclude confounders.

\paragraph{Entropy--diversity coupling.}
Token entropy and distinct-$n$ share mechanical coupling, but this would produce \emph{synchronous} co-movement (lag~0), not the consistent one-generation lead we observe (Figure~\ref{fig:metric_trajectory}).
First-differencing rules out shared trends: canary$\to$diversity survives ($24/24 \to 24/24$) while canary$\to$perplexity vanishes entirely (Appendix~\ref{app:channel_effect_size}).
The temporal lead is formalized in \S\ref{sec:cascade}: entropy is a first-order indicator while diversity requires a threshold crossing, producing a ${\geq}1$-generation lag; partial mechanical coupling cannot be fully excluded.

\paragraph{Detection boundary precision.}
With $T{=}10$ (11 data points), T-invariance at $\alpha \geq 0.50$ (6/8$\to$6/8) confirms that sample size is not the binding constraint~\cite{boettiger2012limits}; $T{=}20$ extends detection to $\alpha{=}0.45$ (0/8$\to$5/8), confirming the power-limited interpretation (\S\ref{sec:theory}).
Sigmoid regression yields $\alpha_c = 0.483$ (bootstrap 95\% CI: $[0.43, 0.56]$); the wide CI reflects the step-function nature of the transition (Appendix~\ref{app:sigmoid_shape}).
This is a statistical detection threshold contingent on method, $T$, and metric choice, not a physical constant; the CMR of \citet{gu2024cmr}---a collapse dynamics threshold---is a distinct concept.

\paragraph{Regime interpretation.}
The non-monotonic dose-response parallels hormetic patterns~\cite{calabrese2003hormesis}; mutual information analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_analysis}) confirms canary--diversity coupling exists continuously across $\alpha$ but falls below PermG's detection floor---a \emph{statistical detection} phenomenon, not a phase transition.

\paragraph{Cross-architecture scope.}
The four non-confirming conditions reflect distinct failure modes: GPT-2-medium (fp32 6/8, bf16 0/8) exhibits precision-dependent detection (\S\ref{sec:generalization}); SmolLM3-3B (3/8) shows entropy--diversity decoupling (entropy 0/4 vs.\ ECE 3/4); Pythia-1.4B exhibits seed-dependent and scale-non-monotonic detection; C4 shows preliminary bidirectional coupling (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative training collapse is far more fragile than assumed: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to 92\% null-control FPR, and regime dependence causes the same test to shift from significant to null.
PermG identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse, validated across 15 model-seed combinations (five families, 345M--6.9B); canary-based cessation reduces peak perplexity by 58\% (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).

\section*{Ethics Statement and AI Writing Disclosure}

\paragraph{AI writing assistance.}
This paper was drafted with assistance from Claude (Anthropic), used for LaTeX formatting, literature search, and iterative revision.
All scientific claims, experimental design decisions, data analysis, and interpretations are the sole responsibility of the authors.
The AI assistant did not design experiments, run code, or make editorial judgments about which results to include or exclude.

\paragraph{Responsible NLP considerations.}
This work studies degradation in iterative language model training.
We do not release models trained on synthetic data, as they exhibit reduced diversity and calibration.
Our findings highlight a failure mode relevant to the responsible deployment of language models: unmonitored iterative training on web-crawled data (which increasingly contains LLM outputs) may degrade model quality in ways detectable only through the multi-method framework we propose.
We encourage practitioners to implement canary monitoring in production training pipelines as a safeguard against silent quality degradation.

